\newtheorem{lemma}{Lemma}
\begin{document}
\title{Formal Derivation of Monotonic Subsequence Bound}
\maketitle
\begin{abstract}
We present a rigorous combinatorial proof utilizing coordinate pair labeling.
\end{abstract}
\section{Definitions and Coordinate Assignment}
Let $S = (x_1, x_2, \dots, x_{n^2+1})$ be a sequence of distinct real numbers. For each index $i \in \{1, 2, \dots, n^2+1\}$, we define $a_i \ge 1$ to be the length of the longest increasing subsequence of $S$ that terminates at $x_i$, and $b_i \ge 1$ to be the length of the longest decreasing subsequence of $S$ that terminates at $x_i$. Since the single-element sequence $(x_i)$ is both trivially increasing and decreasing, we immediately have $a_i \ge 1$ and $b_i \ge 1$ for all $i$.
\section{Strict Invariant and Injectivity}
Let $1 \le i < j \le n^2+1$. Since all elements in $S$ are distinct by hypothesis, we have either $x_i < x_j$ or $x_i > x_j$.\n\n\textbf{Case 1 ($x_i < x_j$):} Any increasing subsequence terminating at $x_i$ can be extended by appending $x_j$, yielding an increasing subsequence terminating at $x_j$ of length $a_i + 1$. Consequently, $a_j \ge a_i + 1 > a_i$.\n\n\textbf{Case 2 ($x_i > x_j$):} By identical symmetric reasoning, any decreasing subsequence terminating at $x_i$ can be extended by appending $x_j$, yielding a decreasing subsequence terminating at $x_j$ of length $b_i + 1$. Thus $b_j \ge b_i + 1 > b_i$.\n\nIn either case, at least one coordinate strictly increases, which guarantees that $(a_i, b_i) \neq (a_j, b_j)$. Hence the mapping $i \mapsto (a_i, b_i)$ is strictly injective.
\section{Pigeonhole Dimension Contradiction and Conclusion}
Suppose, for the sake of contradiction, that $S$ contains no increasing subsequence of length $n+1$ and no decreasing subsequence of length $n+1$. Then for every $i \in \{1, 2, \dots, n^2+1\}$, we must have $1 \le a_i \le n$ and $1 \le b_i \le n$. The set of all possible coordinate pairs is therefore a subset of the Cartesian product $\{1, 2, \dots, n\} \times \{1, 2, \dots, n\}$, which contains exactly $n \cdot n = n^2$ distinct elements.\n\nHowever, by Lemma 2, the mapping $i \mapsto (a_i, b_i)$ is strictly injective, meaning each of the $n^2+1$ elements of $S$ is mapped to a unique pair. By the Pigeonhole Principle, $n^2+1$ distinct elements cannot be injectively mapped into a set of size $n^2$. This contradiction completes the proof: $S$ must contain at least one monotonic subsequence of length $n+1$.
\end{document}

